2026-08-16T12:15:34Z    [HAS:Net fees] GATE critic: The base you use is a unit/scale misread: “prior-year same period H1 2025 (-1400)” improperly treats a “(1.4)” in the H1 FY25 table (e.g., Australia & New Zealand 74.3 (1.4) …) as -1,400 GBPm of net fees. That “(1.4)” is a percentage change (−1.4%), not an amount, and Hays’ H1 FY25 Group net fees are positive and in the low hundreds of £m, not negative £1.4bn. With the base off by orders of magnitude and wrong sign, the derived FY2026 276.044 GBPm is invalid.
2026-08-16T12:15:39Z    [HD:Net sales] GATE critic: Arithmetic mismatch with your own inputs: 45,300 (USDm) × (1 + 4.86%) = 47,501.58 (USDm), not 47,502.1. The proposed value is overstated by ~0.5 USDm.
2026-08-16T12:16:07Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional basic EPS] GATE critic: The 1.31p anchor you used for FY2025 is almost certainly the half-year (H1 FY2025) pre-exceptional basic EPS from the interim results (shown as 1.31p vs 4.03p, -67%), not the full-year FY2025 figure. Using an H1 number to project a full-year FY2026 EPS is a period mismatch and will understate the base. Check the FY2025 full-year pre-exceptional basic EPS in the FY2025 annual results/RNS instead of the H1 1.31p.
2026-08-16T12:16:15Z    [DE:Worldwide net sales and revenues] GATE critic: You anchored the trend on a misstated prior-year change: Deere reported FY2025Q3 worldwide net sales and revenues down 9% to $12,018m, but you used −8.6% for FY2024Q3→FY2025Q3. Using the reported −9% (for the same metric and period) would steepen your averaged decline and lower the FY2026Q3 estimate by roughly $25–30m, so 10,496.9 USDm is biased high off an incorrect basis.
2026-08-16T12:16:27Z    [HD:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE critic: You’re projecting FY2026Q2 by applying a +0.21% YoY change inferred from FY2024Q2→FY2025Q2 EPS moving from $4.67 to $4.68, but those EPS inputs are rounded to the cent. Using two-decimal rounded figures to compute a sub‑percentage growth is numerically meaningless: the true YoY change could range from ~0.00% (e.g., $4.675/$4.675) to ~0.43% (e.g., $4.685/$4.665). Multiplying $4.68 by this rounding‑noise rate to get 4.69002 is an unsupported basis for FY2026Q2 and over-precise relative to how EPS is reported.
2026-08-16T12:16:39Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional operating profit] GATE critic: You used £45.6m as the “prior-year FY2025” base, but £45.6m is H1 FY2025 pre-exceptional operating profit (vs £105.1m in H1 FY2024, -57%), not a full-year FY2025 figure. Building an FY2026 full-year forecast off a half-year base is the wrong fiscal period/basis and will understate the result.
2026-08-16T12:16:40Z  [HAS] wrote submission\HAS-FY2026.xlsx
2026-08-16T12:16:40Z  [HAS] wrote research\HAS.md
2026-08-16T12:16:52Z    [DE:Diluted EPS (GAAP)] GATE critic: Wrong basis: You anchored FY2026Q3 to FY2024Q3’s GAAP diluted EPS of $6.29 and applied a two-year compounded growth to reach ~$8.43 before the tilt. The correct like-for-like comparable for FY2026Q3 is FY2025Q3, not FY2024Q3. Using 2024Q3 introduces an extra year of growth and mismatches the fiscal period, so the $8.2982 figure is on the wrong basis. This is checkable against Deere’s fiscal calendar and reported quarterly EPS (FY2026Q3 should be compared year-over-year to FY2025Q3).
2026-08-16T12:17:14Z    [HD:Comparable sales, total company] GATE critic: Wrong basis: the inputs mix U.S.-only comps with total-company comps. Your cited FY2025Q2 source explicitly says “comparable sales … increased 1.0%, and … in the U.S. increased 1.4%.” Any seasonal/trend adjustment that uses the 1.4% U.S. figure (and the FY2024Q2 table’s second value, (2.0)%, which likely refers to U.S. as well) is not on a total-company basis. For a total-company FY2026Q2 estimate you must use the total-company comps (e.g., 1.0% for FY2025Q2), not the U.S.-only figures; otherwise the 2.21608% result is on the wrong basis.
2026-08-16T12:17:14Z  [HD] wrote submission\HD-FY2026Q2.xlsx
2026-08-16T12:17:14Z  [HD] wrote research\HD.md
2026-08-16T12:17:32Z    [DE:Production & Precision Ag operating profit] GATE critic: The -0.17% YoY change you apply to FY2025Q3 ($580m) is not the FY2025Q3→FY2026Q3 growth; it’s an average of two unrelated periods (FY2024Q3→FY2025Q3 −50.1% and FY2020Q3→FY2021Q3 +49.8%). Averaging those non‑contiguous swings to proxy FY2026Q3 is a wrong basis and effectively just carries forward FY2025Q3 (~$580m) with no justification tied to the actual FY2025→FY2026 period.
